Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• player.player_id : primary key of each player – join anchor for all player–related calculations.  
• player.country_name : country to which the player belongs – final aggregation will be done on this field.  

• ball_by_ball.match_id / over_id / ball_id / innings_no : together with batsman_scored they identify every legal delivery.  
• ball_by_ball.striker : id of the batsman who faced the ball – lets us know *who* scored the runs.  

• batsman_scored.match_id / over_id / ball_id / innings_no : composite key matching ball_by_ball – lets us join the two tables.  
• batsman_scored.runs_scored : runs credited to the striker on that ball – the basic measure we need.  

Typical join condition (to attach the scorer to the runs)  
ON  bb.match_id  = bs.match_id
AND bb.over_id   = bs.over_id
AND bb.ball_id   = bs.ball_id
AND bb.innings_no= bs.innings_no

10 step‑by‑step exploratory queries
(Each query returns at most 20 rows, uses DISTINCT whenever helpful, and is preceded by a short comment.)

```sql
-- 1. List the different countries represented in the data set
SELECT DISTINCT country_name
FROM player
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Pick a few players together with their countries
SELECT player_id,
       player_name,
       country_name
FROM player
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. See some striker ids that appear in ball_by_ball
SELECT DISTINCT striker
FROM ball_by_ball
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Inspect some scoring events worth six runs
SELECT match_id,
       over_id,
       ball_id,
       innings_no,
       runs_scored
FROM batsman_scored
WHERE runs_scored = 6
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Join a delivery with its striker to verify the linking keys
SELECT bb.match_id,
       bb.over_id,
       bb.ball_id,
       bb.striker   AS player_id,
       bs.runs_scored
FROM ball_by_ball AS bb
JOIN batsman_scored AS bs
  ON  bb.match_id   = bs.match_id
  AND bb.over_id    = bs.over_id
  AND bb.ball_id    = bs.ball_id
  AND bb.innings_no = bs.innings_no
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Compute total runs a player scored in individual matches (sample)
SELECT bb.striker                AS player_id,
       bs.match_id,
       SUM(bs.runs_scored)       AS runs_in_match
FROM batsman_scored AS bs
JOIN ball_by_ball  AS bb
  ON  bb.match_id   = bs.match_id
  AND bb.over_id    = bs.over_id
  AND bb.ball_id    = bs.ball_id
  AND bb.innings_no = bs.innings_no
GROUP BY bb.striker, bs.match_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Derive every player's average runs per match (sample)
WITH player_runs AS (
    SELECT bb.striker AS player_id,
           bs.match_id,
           SUM(bs.runs_scored) AS runs_in_match
    FROM batsman_scored AS bs
    JOIN ball_by_ball  AS bb
      ON  bb.match_id   = bs.match_id
      AND bb.over_id    = bs.over_id
      AND bb.ball_id    = bs.ball_id
      AND bb.innings_no = bs.innings_no
    GROUP BY bb.striker, bs.match_id
)
SELECT player_id,
       AVG(runs_in_match) AS avg_runs_per_match
FROM player_runs
GROUP BY player_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Attach country information to every player's batting average
WITH player_runs AS (
    SELECT bb.striker AS player_id,
           bs.match_id,
           SUM(bs.runs_scored) AS runs_in_match
    FROM batsman_scored AS bs
    JOIN ball_by_ball  AS bb
      ON  bb.match_id   = bs.match_id
      AND bb.over_id    = bs.over_id
      AND bb.ball_id    = bs.ball_id
      AND bb.innings_no = bs.innings_no
    GROUP BY bb.striker, bs.match_id
),
player_avg AS (
    SELECT player_id,
           AVG(runs_in_match) AS avg_runs_per_match
    FROM player_runs
    GROUP BY player_id
)
SELECT p.country_name,
       pa.player_id,
       pa.avg_runs_per_match
FROM player_avg AS pa
JOIN player      AS p
  ON p.player_id = pa.player_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Compute the country‑level batting average (intermediate result)
WITH player_runs AS (
    SELECT bb.striker AS player_id,
           bs.match_id,
           SUM(bs.runs_scored) AS runs_in_match
    FROM batsman_scored AS bs
    JOIN ball_by_ball  AS bb
      ON  bb.match_id   = bs.match_id
      AND bb.over_id    = bs.over_id
      AND bb.ball_id    = bs.ball_id
      AND bb.innings_no = bs.innings_no
    GROUP BY bb.striker, bs.match_id
),
player_avg AS (
    SELECT player_id,
           AVG(runs_in_match) AS avg_runs_per_match
    FROM player_runs
    GROUP BY player_id
)
SELECT p.country_name,
       AVG(pa.avg_runs_per_match) AS country_batting_avg
FROM player_avg AS pa
JOIN player      AS p
  ON p.player_id = pa.player_id
GROUP BY p.country_name
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Final query: Top‑5 countries with the highest country batting average
WITH player_runs AS (
    SELECT bb.striker AS player_id,
           bs.match_id,
           SUM(bs.runs_scored) AS runs_in_match
    FROM batsman_scored AS bs
    JOIN ball_by_ball  AS bb
      ON  bb.match_id   = bs.match_id
      AND bb.over_id    = bs.over_id
      AND bb.ball_id    = bs.ball_id
      AND bb.innings_no = bs.innings_no
    GROUP BY bb.striker, bs.match_id
),
player_avg AS (
    SELECT player_id,
           AVG(runs_in_match) AS avg_runs_per_match
    FROM player_runs
    GROUP BY player_id
),
country_avg AS (
    SELECT p.country_name,
           AVG(pa.avg_runs_per_match) AS country_batting_avg
    FROM player_avg AS pa
    JOIN player      AS p
      ON p.player_id = pa.player_id
    GROUP BY p.country_name
)
SELECT country_name,
       country_batting_avg
FROM country_avg
ORDER BY country_batting_avg DESC
LIMIT 5;
```

These ten queries progress from simple inspections of individual columns to the complete computation of the requested top‑5 country batting averages.
